From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jakub Mendyka <jkmendyka@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:09:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] exynos-cpupm: use the fast monotonic clock for the sleep
 length

get_sleep_length() runs in the idle path. During suspend-to-idle the
last CPU freezes the tick and suspends timekeeping, and the ktime_get()
call then warned on every idle entry ("timekeeping.c:753 ktime_get" from
cpuidle_enter_s2idle). ktime_get_mono_fast_ns() is safe there.
---
 drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-cpupm.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-cpupm.c b/drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-cpupm.c
index 1acd3666c..21fc7a908 100755
--- a/drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-cpupm.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/samsung/exynos-cpupm.c
@@ -409,10 +409,17 @@ void enable_power_mode(int cpu, int type)
 	spin_unlock(&cpupm_lock);
 }
 
-/* get sleep length of given cpu from tickless framework */
+/*
+ * get sleep length of given cpu from tickless framework
+ *
+ * This runs in the idle path, including suspend-to-idle, where
+ * timekeeping is suspended and ktime_get() warns on every call. The fast
+ * monotonic clock is safe there.
+ */
 static s64 get_sleep_length(int cpu)
 {
-	return ktime_to_us(ktime_sub(*(get_next_event_cpu(cpu)), ktime_get()));
+	return ktime_to_us(ktime_sub(*(get_next_event_cpu(cpu)),
+				     ns_to_ktime(ktime_get_mono_fast_ns())));
 }
 
 static int cpus_busy(int target_residency, const struct cpumask *cpus)
